Fix USB RX resubmit deadlock that wedges the xHCI controller on disconnect

When the adapter is unplugged/reset while RX URBs are in flight, usb_submit_urb()
returns -ENODEV, but usb_dev->state can still read USB_UP_ST because the USB core
has not yet run the driver's .disconnect callback. The msg-RX resubmit path did
not cope and spun forever:

  * aicwf_usb_submit_msg_rx_urb() logged the failure, put the buffer back on the
    free list, mdelay(100)'d, and then RETURNED 0 (success). Its caller
    aicwf_usb_msg_rx_submit_all_urb() loops `while ((buf = buf_get()) != NULL)
    submit(buf)` and only bailed on state != USB_UP_ST -- so with the state still
    up it re-fetched the SAME buffer and re-submitted it forever
    ("usb submit msg rx urb fail:-19" at 10 Hz in dmesg).
  * That loop runs in the URB-completion (atomic) path and never returns, so the
    USB core's disconnect for the interface never completes. usb_hub_wq blocks in
    D state holding the device/controller locks, and lsusb / uhubctl /
    xhci_hcd unbind / modprobe -r all hang behind it -- the whole xHCI controller
    wedges until reboot.
  * mdelay(100) in the completion path is a 100 ms busy-wait in atomic context --
    a bug in itself. The data-RX submit twins had the same mdelay (they already
    returned -1, so they broke the loop, but the atomic mdelay was still wrong).

Fix (minimal and self-contained):
  * aicwf_usb_submit_msg_rx_urb() returns -1 on submit failure instead of 0, so
    the caller sees the failure.
  * aicwf_usb_msg_rx_submit_all_urb() stops on ANY submit failure instead of
    re-fetching the same buffer, matching the data-RX path.
  * Remove mdelay(100) from all three submit paths.

Deliberately does NOT touch usb_dev->state from the RX path: the RX-completion
error path can hold aicwf_deinit_sem, and aicwf_usb_bus_stop() only up()s that
semaphore when it does not early-return on state==USB_DOWN_ST -- so forcing the
state down from here would orphan the semaphore and create a different deadlock.
Breaking the resubmit loop is sufficient; the existing teardown path sets the
state down correctly.

This is the resubmit-spin variant of the same "survive the device going away
under load" class as the probe-race / kthread_stop hang fixed separately.

Signed-off-by: (AIC8800D80 out-of-tree fixups)
---
diff --git a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c
index b3e194b..e2e7023 100644
--- a/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c
+++ b/src/USB/driver_fw/drivers/aic8800/aic8800_fdrv/aicwf_usb.c
@@ -782,9 +782,7 @@ static int aicwf_usb_submit_rx_urb(struct aic_usb_dev *usb_dev,
         usb_unanchor_urb(usb_buf->urb);
         aicwf_prealloc_rxbuff_free(rx_buff, &usb_dev->rx_priv->rxbuff_lock);
         aicwf_usb_rx_buf_put(usb_dev, usb_buf);
-
-        mdelay(100);
-	    return -1;
+        return -1;
     }else{
     	atomic_inc(&rx_urb_cnt);
 	}
@@ -841,8 +839,6 @@ static int aicwf_usb_submit_rx_urb(struct aic_usb_dev *usb_dev,
         aicwf_dev_skb_free(usb_buf->skb);
         usb_buf->skb = NULL;
         aicwf_usb_rx_buf_put(usb_dev, usb_buf);
-
-        mdelay(100);
         return -1;
     }else{
         atomic_inc(&rx_urb_cnt);
@@ -914,8 +910,7 @@ static int aicwf_usb_submit_msg_rx_urb(struct aic_usb_dev *usb_dev,
         aicwf_dev_skb_free(usb_buf->skb);
         usb_buf->skb = NULL;
         aicwf_usb_msg_rx_buf_put(usb_dev, usb_buf);
-
-        mdelay(100);
+        return -1;
     }
     return 0;
 }
@@ -933,8 +928,16 @@ static void aicwf_usb_msg_rx_submit_all_urb(struct aic_usb_dev *usb_dev)
     while((usb_buf = aicwf_usb_msg_rx_buf_get(usb_dev)) != NULL) {
         if (aicwf_usb_submit_msg_rx_urb(usb_dev, usb_buf)) {
             AICWFDBG(LOGERROR, "usb msg rx refill fail\n");
-            if (usb_dev->state != USB_UP_ST)
-                return;
+            /* Stop on ANY submit failure. The buffer is already back on the free
+             * list, so continuing would re-fetch and re-submit the same buffer
+             * forever. The old code only bailed when the state was already down,
+             * which spun when the device vanished but state still read
+             * USB_UP_ST -- blocking USB disconnect and wedging the whole xHCI
+             * controller until reboot. Do NOT force the state down from here:
+             * the RX-completion error path can hold aicwf_deinit_sem, and
+             * aicwf_usb_bus_stop() only up()s it if it does not early-return on
+             * state==USB_DOWN_ST, so downing the state here would orphan it. */
+            return;
         }
     }
 }
